.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Balandin&#x27;s multiplet theory (1929) predicted that when catalytic activity is plotted against binding strength across a series of catalysts, it rises, peaks and falls again, tracing a &quot;volcano&quot;. Bligaard, Norskov and coworkers (2004) later derived that shape from the Bronsted-Evans-Polanyi relation. This example reproduces the volcano with the standard textbook toy model (Chorkendorff &amp; Niemantsverdriet, Concepts of Modern Catalysis and Kinetics, 2nd ed., Wiley-VCH, 2007, Ch. 8). The rate needs an occupied site to hold the intermediate and an empty site for the next step, so rate \propto\theta(1-\theta), with \theta the Langmuir coverage (~chemistrykit.surface.systems.langmuir.langmuir_coverage). The adsorption constant K stands in for binding strength. A hypothetical series of catalysts sits on the two legs of the volcano, and its summit is exactly Langmuir&#x27;s half-saturation point K = 1/P. This is not a literal reproduction of Balandin&#x27;s multiplet equations.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/surface/catalysis/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_05_balandin_volcano_thumb.png
